=========================
Call to Power II Ruleset
=========================

Goal
----

This ruleset aims for gameplay close to Activision's Call to Power II
(CTP2) while staying a plain Freeciv ruleset (mod) wherever the engine
allows it. It targets the stable Freeciv 3.2 series.

It started as a copy of the sandbox ruleset of Freeciv 3.2.6, so
everything not listed below still behaves like sandbox.

Work in progress: balance is untested and the AI does not understand
all of the mechanics yet (see "Known limitations").


Installing and updating
-----------------------

scripts/ctp2-update.sh (Linux, macOS) and scripts/ctp2-update.ps1
(Windows) download or update the mod from git and copy this ruleset
into the user data directory (~/.freeciv/3.2 or %APPDATA%\.freeciv\3.2),
where any installed Freeciv 3.2.x finds it. Run them again whenever
the mod changes. With --build, ctp2-update.sh also compiles the whole
fork (GTK 4 client by default) into ~/fc-ctp2.

In a new game, type "/rulesetdir ctp2" before starting.


Domestic policies (CTP2 Empire Manager)
---------------------------------------

CTP2 lets you set Rations, Workday and Wages for the whole empire.
They are implemented as multipliers (policies), adjustable from the
client's policies dialog. Every slider goes from -2 to +2, with 0 as
normal. One step moves the base number of content citizens of every
city by one; when it drops below zero, citizens start out angry.

  Rations  +1: one more content citizen, -10% food.
  Workday  +1: one less content citizen, +10% shields.
  Wages    +1: one more content citizen, -10% gold.

Lowering a slider has the opposite effect.


Military readiness
------------------

The Demobilization policy plays the role of CTP2's military readiness:

  0 = At War    (default) full strength, normal upkeep.
  1 = On Alert  every city supports one more unit free of upkeep,
                units fight 25% worse.
  2 = At Peace  two more free units per city, units fight 50% worse.

After changing it you must keep the new value for 5 turns, so you
cannot switch to At War just after an enemy attacks.


Public Works
------------

As in CTP2, tile improvements can be paid for from a national Public
Works pool instead of being built by units. The Public Works Tax
policy (0-50%, default 10%) removes that percentage of shields from
every city. In return each city adds infrapoints every turn: tax/10
points, and the same amount again at sizes 5, 9, 13 and 17.

Infrapoints are spent to place these improvements on tiles inside
your borders:

  Road 10, Railroad 30, Maglev 60, Irrigation 15, Farmland 30,
  Mine 20, Oil Well 40, Fort 15, Fortress 30, Airstrip 25, Airbase 40

The usual technology and terrain requirements still apply. Placing
takes several turns, depending on the terrain.

Placing improvements needs a client with the infrastructure dialog:
the GTK 4 client (and GTK 3.22). The Qt client does not offer it in
Freeciv 3.2.


Technology tree
---------------

The 106 advances of CTP2 replace the Freeciv tech tree, with the CTP2
prerequisites. Research costs still follow Freeciv's "Linear" formula
(cost grows with the number of prerequisites) instead of CTP2's own
numbers, which keeps the research pace close to other Freeciv rulesets.

Units
-----

The combat, naval and air units of CTP2 replace the Freeciv units.
Each one takes from CTP2 its name, enabling advance, upgrade path,
cost, attack, defense, movement and vision, converted like this:

  attack, defense   CTP2 value / 5 (ranged units use their ranged
                    attack as attack)
  hit points        10 x CTP2 armor
  firepower         as in CTP2
  build cost        CTP2 shield cost / 15
  vision radius     CTP2 range r gives vision_radius_sq r*r + 1

Freeciv specific behavior (unit class, abilities, cargo, roles used
by the AI) comes from the closest sandbox unit.

Some Freeciv units remain because the engine or the AI needs them:
Workers, Engineers, Mechanic, Explorer, Partisan, Fanatics, Leader,
Barbarian Leader and Storm.

Not converted yet: undersea and space units (Sea Engineer, Crawler,
Space Plane) and the Cargo Helicopter. The Fascist is available (see
Governments) and the special units are described below.


Special units
-------------

The CTP2 special units use Freeciv actions; script.lua adds what the
engine lacks. Cost, movement and vision follow the unit conversion
above; like the Diplomat they have no attack or defense.

  Cleric          Theology, Theocracy only. "Convert City" on a
                  foreign city.
  Televangelist   Mass Media, Theocracy only. Converts like the
                  Cleric, with a bigger tithe. Replaces the Cleric.
  Corporate       Corporation. "Create Franchise" on a foreign city.
    Branch
  Lawyer          Criminal Code. Injunction ("Sabotage City
                  Production") against foreign cities, and sues
                  ("Expel Unit") foreign Televangelists, Corporate
                  Branches and Lawyers out of your territory.
  Slaver          Slave Labor. "Slave Raid" on an enemy city of size
                  2 or more: one citizen less there, and new Workers
                  for you. Captures enemy Settlers, Urban Planners,
                  Workers and Engineers in the field.
  Abolitionist    Classical Education. Catches Slavers that raid the
                  city it is in, and expels foreign Slavers from
                  your territory.
  Eco-Ranger      Ecotopia, Ecotopia only. Turns an enemy city into
                  a park ("Destroy City"); the Eco-Ranger is used up.
  Eco-Terrorist   Ecotopia, Ecotopia only. Nano terror (suitcase
                  nuke) against a foreign city.
  Infector        Nano-Warfare. Spreads plague into a foreign city of
                  size 5 or more, or poisons it (one citizen less).

Converting and franchising succeed 75% of the time, as in CTP2; after
a failure the unit dies half of the time. Each one takes the rest of
the unit's turn. While a city is converted, its owner pays the
converter a tithe every turn: city size x 2/5 gold for a Cleric and
x 4/5 for a Televangelist (CTP2 sends 20% and 40% of the city's trade).
A franchise pays its owner city size / 5 gold (at least 1) every turn
from the city owner's treasury (CTP2 takes 10% of the production).
The city owner ends a conversion by keeping one of their own Clerics
or Televangelists in the city at the start of a turn, and a franchise
the same way with a Lawyer.

Slave raids and injunctions follow the "diplchance" server setting
and can fail; the Slaver comes back after a successful raid. The AI
does not build special units yet.


Buildings and wonders
---------------------

The 51 city improvements and 30 wonders of CTP2 replace the Freeciv
ones. Requirements (advance, prerequisite buildings, coastal cities),
cost (CTP2 / 10) and upkeep (CTP2 / 3) come from CTP2, and their
effects are converted from the CTP2 data:

  food, production, science, commerce %   Output_Bonus (commerce
                                          gives gold and luxury)
  happiness                               content citizens
  overcrowding level                      1 content citizen per 4
  maximum population (Aqueduct,           +8 maximum city size each
    Arcologies, Aqua-Filter)
  crime reduction                         less waste (3 x CTP2 %)
  tower bonuses against land, sea, air    defense bonus against
                                          those unit classes
  City Wall, Forcefield                   defense bonus
  pollution reduction                     less pollution
  Anti-Ballistic Missiles                 protection from nukes

Wonders that place a building in every city (Hagia Sophia, Internet,
National Shield, Central Matter Decompiler) give that building's
effects to every city of the owner. Effects without a direct Freeciv
equivalent are approximated: trade route gold becomes +50% trade route
income, regard becomes AI love, GlobeSat reveals the map, The Agency
reveals all cities, and the Solaris Project enables the spaceship
and the Gaia Controller (see below).

The Capitol is the old Palace: every civilization starts with one,
and rebuilding it after losing the capital needs Feudalism, as in
CTP2. Coinage and the spaceship parts also remain.


Governments
-----------

The 12 governments of CTP2 replace the Freeciv ones: Anarchy, Tyranny
(starting government), Monarchy, Republic, Theocracy, Communism,
Fascism, Democracy, Corporate Republic, Technocracy, Ecotopia and
Virtual Democracy, each enabled by its CTP2 advance.

Basic rules such as unit upkeep, celebration and senate come from the
closest sandbox government (Tyranny from Despotism, Theocracy from
Fundamentalism; Fascism from Communism; Technocracy from Republic;
Corporate Republic, Ecotopia and Virtual Democracy from Democracy).
On top of that, these values come from the CTP2 government data:

  maximum science rate          maximum tax/science/luxury rate
  "too many cities" threshold   cities before empire size unhappiness
                                (threshold / 1.5)
  crime                         waste: 20 x coefficient % trade,
                                10 x coefficient % shields
  martial law                   content per unit and maximum units
  war discontent per unit       unhappiness from aggressive units
  food, production, gold and    +/- half of the CTP2 bonus
    science coefficients
  pollution coefficient         more or less pollution from shields

The Fascist unit can only be built under Fascism.


City radius
-----------

As in CTP2, a new city only works the 8 tiles around it and its area
grows with its size. CTP2 grows the radius at 6, 18, 32 and 46
citizens; with this ruleset's smaller maximum city size the steps come
at sizes 3, 10, 17 and 25 (radius_sq 2, 5, 13, 17 and 20).


Gaia Controller victory
-----------------------

Researching Gaia Controller allows building the Solaris Project
wonder. Once it exists anywhere in the world, every civilization with
the advance can build:

  Gaia Controller Core   one per civilization (small wonder)
  Gaia Power Satellite   city improvement, +10% shields in its city
  Processing Tower       tile improvement (Workers or Public Works)

A civilization that has the Core, satellites in at least 10 cities and
at least 5 Processing Towers inside its borders covering 60% of the map
starts the Gaia Controller; every player is told. Each tower covers the
tiles within 5 tiles, growing to 16 tiles as satellites go from 10 to
40. If the conditions hold for 10 consecutive turns the civilization
wins the game; if they break, the countdown starts again. This is
implemented in script.lua. The Freeciv spaceship is still available as
an alternative science victory.


Feats of Wonder
---------------

As in CTP2, the first civilization to accomplish a feat gets a bonus
for some turns. Nobody else can get that feat afterwards.

  Feat                      Accomplished by                Turns  Bonus
  Concrete                  first to discover Concrete      15  +5% shields
  Gunpowder                 first to discover Gunpowder     25  +5% attack
  Mass Production           first to discover it            15  +5% shields
  Computer                  first to discover Computer      15  +5% science
  Robotics                  first to discover Robotics      15  +5% shields
  Life Extension            first to discover it            15  2 content
  Theaters                  Theater in 8 cities             15  5 content
  Brokerages                Brokerage in 10 cities          20  +10% gold
  Internet                  Computer Center in 12 cities    25  +10% science
  Syndicate                 Television in 15 cities         25  5 content
  Orbital Labs              Orbital Laboratory in 20 cities 25  +10% science
  Sailed Around the World   a ship visits every column of   25  +1 move for
                            a map that wraps east-west          ships
  Conquered by Force        taking the last city of a       25  +10% hit
                            civilization that once had          point
                            5 cities                            recovery
  City Recaptured           recapturing a city you founded  10  1 content

Content bonuses apply to every city. CTP2 makes enemy city walls less
effective after Gunpowder; here the attack bonus stands in for that.
For Conquered by Force, CTP2 adds hit points; here units heal faster.

The bonus is a "Feat: ..." small wonder that script.lua keeps in the
capital of the civilization while the feat lasts; it can not be built.
Progress towards sailing around the world is lost when a game is
reloaded.


Known limitations
-----------------

- Workers are still available, and both the AI and human players can
  use them besides Public Works. With the server of this fork (see
  "Installing and updating", --build), the AI also spends its
  infrapoints on the tile improvements it values most per point and
  sets the Public Works Tax accordingly. A stock Freeciv 3.2 server's
  AI never spends infrapoints.
- The AI does not build or use the special units.
- CTP2 armies (stacks of up to 12 units fighting together, with
  flanking and ranged rows) need engine changes and are not part of
  this ruleset.


Roadmap
-------

1. Policies and Public Works (done).
2. CTP2 technology tree, units, special units, buildings, wonders,
   governments, city radius and Gaia Controller victory (done).
3. Feats of Wonder (done). The readiness delay is the
   minimum time of the Demobilization policy, and CTP2 ruins are
   Freeciv huts.
4. Engine work in this fork: AI use of Public Works (this version),
   then armies and CTP2 combat.
